/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:151:The native `read` tool reads images from the task checkout or an absolute path and sends them as attachments. PNG, JPEG, GIF, and WebP files pass through unchanged within the model's image limits, normally 2000×2000 pixels and 4.5 MiB of base64 data. Images persist in the native journal and checkpoints. Larger images and BMP files return tool diagnostics; resize or convert them before reading. Shurik uses the released Pi Durable coding tools directly, without a custom reader or image processor. A listed model without image input receives Pi AI's placeholder and a diagnostic; an unlisted model receives the attachment so its provider can validate support. No additional action input or credential is needed.
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:155:Pi AI reads its native environment variables and ambient cloud credentials automatically. GitHub secrets must be exposed as environment variables in the iteration action; Shurik needs no new credential input. For OpenAI:
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:175:| `amazon-bedrock` | AWS credentials/profile/role configured on the runner |
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:178:Start, stop, resume, and recovery need the repository token but make no model requests. Keep credentials outside dispatch inputs and saved control/session data. Shurik does not run interactive OAuth login or configure a persistent credential store. Custom providers/endpoints need a worker integration; see Pi AI's [custom-provider documentation](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/ai/README.md#custom-providers). The public demo uses `opencode-go` / `deepseek-v4.1-flash` with native environment authentication.
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:235:Shurik uses three directories under `$HOME/.shurik`: `state/` for sessions, `logs/` for worker and recovery diagnostics, and `emergency/` for fallback snapshots. They are outside every checkout and need no ignore rules, including no blanket `*.log` rule. Worker output is retained as bounded logs in diagnostic JSON files under `logs/`. Set `SHURIK_HOME` to override `$HOME/.shurik` without changing persisted paths. Repository and loop names isolate sessions. Shurik copies the session directory to control-branch `state/` and the runtime logs to `state/diagnostics/` on every checkpoint. The next run restores them into the separate runtime `state/` and `logs/` directories. The persisted version-1 paths stay compatible with older sessions. The task branch is `<loop-id>`; every checkpoint atomically pushes both branch refs and records the task commit in `control.json.checkpoint.workCommit`. A rejected push publishes neither half, and retries preserve stop/resume decisions. Earlier loops retain their recorded task/control refs and remain readable, resumable, and discoverable by recovery; new loops use the names above.
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:241:Temporary engine/build/worker-I/O directories live under `RUNNER_TEMP` (locally, the OS temporary directory). They are not session storage. After a supervisor failure, `emergency/<owner>/<repo>/<loop-id>/<run-id>-<attempt>/` under `$HOME/.shurik` contains `workspace/`, `state/`, and `logs/` copies. The workflow uploads that path as evidence when possible; it is not an automatic recovery source. Repository metadata, dependencies, and build output are excluded, and known credentials are redacted before copying.
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:251:Checks cover native journal persistence, fresh contexts, coding/history tools, checkpoint races, failure recovery, model reasoning, and an unrelated consumer repository. The scripted runner proof covers the legacy self-repair path. No real model credentials are needed for these checks.
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:261:**Secrets require separate handling.** Keep credentials in Actions secrets, never in prompts, committed state, or diagnostics. Publication redacts known credentials, common encoded forms, and recognizable token patterns. This is a limited accidental-exposure check, not universal secret detection. GitHub log masking does not sanitize commits. If a credential is exposed, rotate it rather than relying on deleting the visible file. The runner sends task context to the selected model provider.
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:263:**Contributor access and execution are different boundaries.** GitHub normally withholds repository secrets from fork pull request workflows. Keep contributor CI secret-free, and reserve credentialed loop execution for trusted, maintainer-authorized code. Do not execute unreviewed fork code or consume its artifacts in privileged recovery workflows. A repository writer or code executed with a secret can expose it, even though the stored value is not displayed in repository settings. Passing tests does not establish trust in a contributor's changes or prevent an automatically updated runner from leaking a key it receives. [Actions secrets](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/write-workflows/choose-what-workflows-do/use-secrets), [Secure workflow guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use)
/home/runner/work/_actions/igor-makarov/shurik/v2/README.md:265:**The agent shares the runner and its credentials.** Coding tools and candidate code run directly on the Actions runner, can modify local files and make network requests, and receive the job's repository-scoped GitHub token and model-provider credentials. The worker has no separate filesystem sandbox or resource caps. It can interfere with loop state or use the token outside its working branch. This is an intentional prototype tradeoff; timeouts, publication redaction, fixed candidate checks and fallback copies remain, but they do not provide credential isolation or a security boundary.
